The [[Mossdeep Gym]] specializes in {{type|Psychic}} Pokémon. Psychic-type moves are super effective against {{t|Fighting}}- and {{type|Poison}} Pokémon, while Psychic-type Pokémon are weak to {{t|Bug}}-, {{t|Dark}}-, and {{type|Ghost}} moves.

The Gym features a maze of white floor tiles, each with an arrow design. When stepped on, these tiles launch the challenger along the track in the direction of each arrow until they reach a normal floor tile. The direction of the several red floor tiles can be changed by flipping the corresponding switch. Go northwest from the entrance and flip the switch; this redirects the red tile to the west. Walk northward onto the arrow tiles to battle Psychic Preston. Step on this first red tile to move northeastward to the second, and battle Psychic Maura. Follow the nearby white tiles northward to battle Psychic Samantha, then flip the switch to the west; this redirects the second red tile. Go south, then south again to return to the entrance. Follow the white tiles around the southeast corner to battle Psychic Fritz. Flip the switch to redirect the third red tile, then go northeast to reach a single spot of normal flooring near Psychic Maura. Move southward to reach the northwest part of the Gym, and flip the fourth switch near Psychic Virgil. Go southward to return to the entrance again, and follow the white tiles around the southwest corner to reach the twin [[Gym Leader]]s.

Twins [[Tate and Liza]] send out both of their Pokémon at once, drawing challengers into a [[Double Battle]]. While {{p|Lunatone}} makes better use of [[special move]]s, {{p|Solrock}} is better suited to [[physical move]]s. {{p|Lunatone}}'s {{m|Calm Mind}} raises both its {{stat|Special Attack}} and {{stat|Special Defense}}. This lets it deal increased damage with {{m|Psychic}}, and tends to make it the more threatening of the two Pokémon. Solrock has no physical moves to make use of its higher physical {{stat|Attack}}, but {{m|Flamethrower}} and {{m|Solar Beam|SolarBeam}} let it fight back against {{t|Grass}}- and {{type|Water}} Pokémon. As Lunatone can raise the team's Special Defense with {{m|Light Screen}}, physical moves tend to be more effective than special moves.

